# ==================================================================================================
# Docker build-context exclusions — PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition
# ==================================================================================================
#
# WHY THIS FILE SITS AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT, AND WHY THE BUILD CONTEXT IS THE REPOSITORY ROOT.
# Read this before changing anything below; the single most likely way to break the container build
# is to "simplify" the context and then wonder why restore fails.
#
#   Each service's container definition lives at services/<service-name>/Dockerfile, but every
#   service project references the shared libraries and the generated contract stubs under shared/.
#   A Docker build cannot reach outside its own context, so the context CANNOT be the service
#   directory — it must be the repository root. orchestration/docker-compose.yml therefore declares
#
#       build:
#         context: ..
#         dockerfile: services/<service-name>/Dockerfile
#
#   and it is THIS root-level .dockerignore that Docker consults. A per-service .dockerignore would
#   never be read for a root context and would be a confusing dead file; there must be exactly one
#   of these in the repository.
#
# WHY THE EXCLUSIONS BELOW ARE NOT OPTIONAL. Alongside the .NET tree this repository carries the
# read-only Appeon PowerBuilder 2021 estate that is being decomposed: 39 exported library folders
# holding 544 objects, 40 compiled root libraries, a frozen PowerBuilder 12.5 target, consolidated
# redistribution libraries, and seven closed-source Windows native binaries. Measured on this
# checkout that is roughly 125 MB of the ~130 MB working tree. None of it is a build input for a
# .NET service and none of it can be a runtime input either — the target is Linux containers and
# those binaries are Windows PE images. Without this file every one of those bytes is uploaded to
# the daemon and baked into a layer of all four images for zero benefit.
#
# THIS FILE IS ALSO A SECRETS CONTROL. The repository-wide sweep recorded in docs/SECRETS.md found
# eight in-source credential sites plus three sites embedded in vendored native binaries. Six of the
# eight in-source sites live in the legacy test, demo and browser-harness regions excluded below, and
# all three binary sites are among the native libraries excluded below. Keeping those paths out of
# every build context is a structural, mechanical guarantee rather than a matter of reviewer
# diligence. No credential value is reproduced anywhere in this file, and none may ever be added to
# it — refer to docs/SECRETS.md, which records locators, severity and required action without
# reproducing values.
#
# EXCLUSION IS NOT DELETION. Every legacy path named below is the read-only behavioural oracle for
# parity testing. This file only ever tells Docker not to upload a path. Nothing here deletes, moves,
# renames or rewrites a legacy file, and no such change may ever be made.
#
# PATTERN SEMANTICS, VERIFIED AGAINST A REAL BUILD RATHER THAN ASSUMED. These differ from
# .gitignore and getting them wrong is silent:
#   * Patterns are matched against paths relative to the CONTEXT ROOT. A bare `*.pbl` therefore
#     matches ONLY root-level files, NOT nested ones — which is why every recursive rule below is
#     spelled `**/`.
#   * `**/` matches zero or more leading directories, so a single `**/*.pbl` covers the root level
#     and every depth beneath it. No separate root-level duplicate is needed.
#   * A trailing-slash directory pattern excludes that directory's entire subtree.
#   * `!` re-includes, and the LAST matching pattern wins — which is why the deliberate re-inclusion
#     guards are the final section of this file.
#   * Excluding the container definition and this file is safe: Docker still sends both to the
#     builder because it needs them to run the build, while COPY instructions do not pick them up.
#
# RELATIONSHIP TO .gitignore. The two files have different jobs: .gitignore governs what is
# committed, this file governs what is uploaded to the Docker daemon. .gitignore is NOT mirrored
# here, and this refactor makes no change to it. One trap in particular: .gitignore lists /pack/*,
# yet pack/ is physically present and tracked in this checkout, so pack/ is excluded explicitly
# below. Do not assume a git ignore rule keeps anything out of a build context — it does not.
# Conversely bin/ and obj/ are NOT git-ignored in this repository, so excluding them here is what
# stops stale host build output from poisoning an in-container restore.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The read-only PowerBuilder legacy tree
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The behavioural oracle. Read as specification by humans and by parity tests on the host; never a
# build input, never shipped.
ws_objects/
oldversion/
pack/

# Named for completeness. Neither exists in this checkout, but both appear in .gitignore, so a
# working copy may materialise them and they must not reach a build context if it does.
packuse/
bak/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Compiled PowerBuilder artifacts and workspace descriptors, at every depth
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Libraries, dynamic libraries, targets, workspaces and resource manifests. The `**/` form is
# required: the root holds 40 *.pbl plus *.pbt/*.pbw descriptors, and oldversion/125/ holds three
# more descriptors that a root-anchored pattern would miss.
**/*.pbl
**/*.pbd
**/*.pbt
**/*.pbw
**/*.pbr

# Zero *.pbg files exist anywhere in this repository. The pattern is carried deliberately so the
# exclusion list matches the read-only boundary exactly as that boundary is stated, and so a future
# PowerBuilder export that emits library-group files is covered without anyone having to notice.
**/*.pbg

# Named explicitly for legibility even though **/*.pbr already covers it: the resource manifest that
# binds the fonts and images under res/.
res.pbr

# PowerBuilder per-user options and developer scratch objects.
**/*.usr.opt
**/pfw.dev.*

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Closed-source native binaries
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Seven Windows PE libraries, ~46 MB combined, with no source anywhere in the repository. They are
# unusable in a Linux container by construction, and three of them are the binary credential sites
# recorded in docs/SECRETS.md. Named individually so the intent is legible at a glance, then caught
# again by a recursive glob.
pfw.dll
pfwx.dll
blink.dll
blinkfast.dll
sciter.dll
sqlite3.dll
sqlite3.cipher.dll

# Belt and braces. Verified safe: the only *.dll files in this repository are the seven above. The
# .NET build produces its own assemblies INSIDE the container from source, so no host-side *.dll is
# ever a build input, and excluding them all also stops a stale host-built assembly from being
# mistaken for a fresh one.
**/*.dll

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Legacy assets, samples and browser-harness fixtures
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
res/
samples/
sciter_control/

# tests/ IS NOT A GREENFIELD DIRECTORY, and this distinction must survive every future edit of this
# file. The three directories named below are pre-existing read-only legacy browser-harness assets
# and part of the behavioural oracle; tests/blink/ in particular holds one of the in-source
# credential sites. tests/e2e/ beside them is new and purely additive, and is excluded separately in
# section 9 for an entirely different reason. They are listed individually, and never as a blanket
# `tests/`, so that no reader can mistake this directory for empty or safe to clean.
tests/blink/
tests/sciter/
tests/webview/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Version control and continuous integration metadata
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .git/ is by far the largest single win here and is never a build input. Excluding it does mean
# source-link style git metadata is unavailable in-container, which is intended: reproducible builds
# are configured through Directory.Build.props, not by shipping history into a layer.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/

# Tooling scratch directory. Never tracked and never a build input, but it does appear in a working
# tree during automated runs and holds captured screenshots and logs, so a broad COPY would bake it
# into a layer. Excluded so the context stays identical whether or not it happens to be present.
blitzy/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Documentation and other non-build content
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docs/ carries the five read-only legacy specifications plus the seven authored .NET documents. All
# are for humans; no container step consumes any of them.
docs/

# Root-level markdown only — this pattern is root-anchored, exactly as intended. It covers README.md
# and the ~50 KB legacy changelog. Nested markdown is deliberately left alone here so that a
# markdown file which ever becomes a genuine build input (a package readme, for instance) is not
# swept up invisibly by a rule written today.
*.md

# LICENSE and NOTICE are DELIBERATELY NOT EXCLUDED. See the re-inclusion guards in section 12 for
# the reasoning; they are named there rather than here so the decision is impossible to miss.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Orchestration, environment files and credential material
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The whole orchestration directory is excluded. Nothing in it is a build input: the compose manifest
# is read by Docker Compose from the host filesystem, not from the context — verified against a real
# `docker compose build` with `context: ..`. Excluding the directory outright is what structurally
# guarantees that the operator-created orchestration/.env, which holds the one real signing secret in
# the system, can never enter any build context or image layer. The tracked orchestration/.env.example
# is covered by the same rule and stays excluded too: it is a template, no image needs it, and its
# absence removes any chance of it being picked up and read as real configuration.
orchestration/

# Belt and braces for environment files anywhere in the tree, including any a developer creates
# locally. Note there is deliberately no `!**/.env.example` re-inclusion: example templates are not
# needed in an image either.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Belt and braces for key, certificate and keystore material by extension. None of these files
# exists in the repository today and none ever should; the rules make an accidental future addition
# non-shipping by default rather than dependent on someone noticing it in review.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.pfx
**/*.p12
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/*.crt
**/*.cer
**/*.der
**/id_rsa
**/id_ecdsa
**/id_ed25519
**/*.secret
**/secrets.json

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. .NET build output and local tooling state
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Critical, not cosmetic. bin/ and obj/ are NOT git-ignored in this repository, so a host build
# leaves them in the working tree. Uploading a host obj/ lets a stale project.assets.json and stale
# nuget.g.props reach the container, where they can defeat or corrupt the in-container restore. The
# container must always restore from scratch against Directory.Packages.props.
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/out/
**/artifacts/
**/publish/
**/TestResults/
**/coverage.cobertura.xml
**/coverage*.xml
**/coverage*.json
**/*.coverage
**/.vs/
**/.vscode/
**/.idea/
**/*.user
**/*.suo
**/*.userprefs
**/.dotnet/
**/.nuget/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. The end-to-end suite
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tests/e2e/ is the new, purely additive Playwright root that verifies cross-service workflows from
# outside the containers. No service image runs Playwright, so the entire directory — its manifests,
# its specs, its installed packages and its reports — is build-context noise. This is a separate
# decision from the read-only legacy harness directories in section 4 and must stay separate.
tests/e2e/

# Any Node package tree anywhere, in case one is ever created outside tests/e2e/.
**/node_modules/
**/.npm/
**/npm-debug.log*
**/playwright-report/
**/blob-report/
**/test-results/
**/.playwright/
**/ms-playwright/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. Characterization recordings
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paired legacy-oracle and target-side capture output, plus the workflow definitions and determinism
# masks that drive it. Large, environment-specific, and consumed by the parity harness on the host —
# never by a container build step. If a container step is ever genuinely required to read a workflow
# definition, re-include that one path here explicitly rather than deleting this rule.
characterization/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. Container definitions, archives, logs and operating-system detritus
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Excluding the container definitions and this file is safe and intended. Docker sends both to the
# builder regardless because it needs them to run the build; excluding them only stops a broad COPY
# from baking them into the image. Verified against a real compose build whose Dockerfile was matched
# by the pattern below and which still built successfully.
**/Dockerfile*
**/*.dockerfile
**/docker-compose*.yml
**/docker-compose*.yaml
**/compose*.yml
**/compose*.yaml
**/.dockerignore

# Archives. res/ and samples/ hold the repository's only archive files and are already excluded
# wholesale; these rules stop a future one from being uploaded.
**/*.zip
**/*.rar
**/*.7z
**/*.tar
**/*.tar.gz
**/*.tgz

# Windows scripts, crash dumps, logs and editor backups. None is a build input on Linux.
**/*.bat
**/*.cmd
**/*.dmp
**/*.log
**/*.bak
**/*.tmp
**/*.swp
**/*~

# Operating-system metadata.
**/[Tt]humbs.db
**/.DS_Store
**/desktop.ini

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. Deliberate re-inclusions — the build-critical allowlist
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `!` rules come last because the last matching pattern wins. None of the paths below is matched by
# any rule above today; each is asserted anyway, as a standing guard, so that a future broadening of
# a rule in this file cannot silently remove a build input. Every one of these is required for a
# clean in-container restore and build, and losing any of them is the single most likely way to break
# this file. If a container build ever fails to restore, suspect an over-broad exclusion above and
# NARROW it — never respond by weakening central package management.
#
# Deliberately NOT re-included in bulk: whole directories such as shared/ or services/. Those trees
# are already fully included by default, and a blanket `!shared/` would be a standing invitation to
# re-admit the bin/ and obj/ output that section 8 exists to keep out.

# Solution and repository-wide MSBuild plumbing. Directory.Build.props supplies net10.0, nullable,
# implicit usings and warnings-as-errors to all twenty projects; Directory.Packages.props is the
# single source of truth for every package version including the two mandatory security pins;
# global.json pins the SDK. Without any one of these the in-container build either fails outright or,
# worse, silently builds under different settings than the host and CI.
!PowerFramework.slnx
!Directory.Build.props
!Directory.Packages.props
!global.json
!NuGet.config
!nuget.config

# The localization resource table. THIS IS A BUILD INPUT, NOT LEGACY BALLAST, and excluding it is a
# hard build break rather than a size optimisation.
#
# The legacy providers load this table by bare relative filename from the process working directory,
# so the .NET port must place a copy beside the built assembly to reproduce that lookup. The
# localization projects therefore carry a literal, non-glob Content item that copies this exact path
# to the output directory. Verified on this toolchain: with the file absent that item fails the build
# with MSB3030, "could not copy ... because it was not found"; with it present the build reports zero
# warnings and zero errors and the 6,824-byte table lands in the output directory byte for byte.
#
# A second, quieter reason to be careful: the localization fallback is a SILENT PASSTHROUGH — with no
# provider installed the source text is returned unchanged, never throwing and never logging. So if
# this file were excluded and the copy item were ever loosened to a glob, localization would fail
# silently in the container instead of failing loudly. Never exclude this path.
!pfw.i18n.xml

# Editor and analyzer configuration. This is a build input, not a preference file: it is where the
# analyzer severity overrides for the deliberately preserved SCREAMING_SNAKE constant identifiers are
# scoped, because Directory.Build.props sets warnings-as-errors with the .NET analyzers enabled and
# deliberately declines to suppress anything globally. Keeping it in the context is what makes the
# in-container build diagnostically identical to the host and CI build, and it costs a few kilobytes.
#
# Stated precisely rather than overstated, because the distinction matters to anyone auditing this
# rule: as the analyzer set stands today the tree still builds clean without this file, so it is not
# yet load-bearing. It is re-included because it CONFIGURES DIAGNOSTICS, and a context that omits it
# lets the image build under different severities than the host and CI — the "builds on my machine,
# fails in the image" class of defect, which appears the moment a severity here is raised or code
# style enforcement is turned on. Keep it in the context; do not wait for the first failure.
!.editorconfig

# Attribution material, kept available so a container definition can COPY it into the image.
#
# The project is BSD 2-Clause. Its binary-redistribution condition requires that the copyright
# notice, the list of conditions and the disclaimer be reproduced in the materials provided with the
# distribution, and a container image is a binary distribution. NOTICE additionally carries the
# four-condition restatement from the project readme and the eleven upstream attributions the
# framework depends on. Directory.Build.props already advertises both files in the assembly copyright
# metadata, so an image that omitted them would be pointing at material it does not carry. They total
# a few kilobytes; the cost of excluding them is a failed COPY or an unsatisfied licence obligation.
!LICENSE
!NOTICE
